{"name":"Home Brew","hex":"#897b66","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"home brew","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of homebrew."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/home_brew"}],"entries":[{"title":"Homebrew Computer Club","blocks":[{"text":"The Homebrew Computer Club was an early computer hobbyist group in Menlo Park, California, which met from March 1975 to December 1986. The club had an influential role in the development of the microcomputer revolution and the rise of that aspect of the Silicon Valley information technology industrial complex."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Gordon_French_%282013%29.jpg/330px-Gordon_French_%282013%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homebrew_Computer_Club"},{"title":"Homebrew (package manager)","blocks":[{"text":"Homebrew is a free and open-source software package management system that simplifies the installation of software on Apple's operating system, macOS, as well as Linux. The name is intended to suggest the idea of building software on the Mac depending on the user's taste. Originally written by Max Howell, the package manager has gained popularity in the Ruby on Rails community and earned praise for its extensibility. Homebrew uses a beer-themed naming convention for its features; for example, third-party repositories are called \"taps\", and binary packages are called \"bottles\". It has been recommended for its ease of use as well as its integration into the command-line interface. Homebrew is a member of the Open Source Collective, and is run entirely by unpaid volunteers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Homebrew_logo.svg/330px-Homebrew_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homebrew_(package_manager)"},{"title":"Home Brew (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Home Brew, also known as Home Brew Crew, is a New Zealand hip hop group."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Home_Brew%2C_Homegrown_festival%2C_Wellington%2C_2012.jpg/330px-Home_Brew%2C_Homegrown_festival%2C_Wellington%2C_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Home_Brew_(band)"},{"title":"Home Brew (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Home Brew is the first studio album by New Zealand hip-hop group Home Brew. It was released by Young, Gifted & Broke on 1 May 2012. The album draws mainly from the genre of New Zealand hip hop, but is also jazz-influenced. Home Brew was promoted by a 48-hour-long release party and a six-show tour of New Zealand cities. The album was lauded by critics, and won the New Zealand Music Award for Best Urban/Hip Hop Album. It debuted top of the New Zealand Albums Chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7e/Home_Brew_album.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Home_Brew_(album)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Invitation_to_First_Homebrew_Computer_Club_meeting.jpg/500px-Invitation_to_First_Homebrew_Computer_Club_meeting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Invitation_to_First_Homebrew_Computer_Club_meeting.jpg/1280px-Invitation_to_First_Homebrew_Computer_Club_meeting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Invitation_to_First_Homebrew_Computer_Club_meeting.jpg/500px-Invitation_to_First_Homebrew_Computer_Club_meeting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Invitation to first Homebrew Computer Club meeting, sent by Fred Moore to Steve Dompier","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AInvitation_to_First_Homebrew_Computer_Club_meeting.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Gordon_French%2C_Lee_Felsenstein%2C_Harry_Garland_%282017%29.jpg/500px-Gordon_French%2C_Lee_Felsenstein%2C_Harry_Garland_%282017%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Gordon_French%2C_Lee_Felsenstein%2C_Harry_Garland_%282017%29.jpg/1280px-Gordon_French%2C_Lee_Felsenstein%2C_Harry_Garland_%282017%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Gordon_French%2C_Lee_Felsenstein%2C_Harry_Garland_%282017%29.jpg/500px-Gordon_French%2C_Lee_Felsenstein%2C_Harry_Garland_%282017%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gordon French, Lee Felsenstein, and Harry Garland would frequent the Oasis following the formal meetings of the club.[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGordon_French%2C_Lee_Felsenstein%2C_Harry_Garland_(2017).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Roger_Melen_at_the_Oasis_%282018%29.jpg/500px-Roger_Melen_at_the_Oasis_%282018%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Roger_Melen_at_the_Oasis_%282018%29.jpg/1280px-Roger_Melen_at_the_Oasis_%282018%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Roger_Melen_at_the_Oasis_%282018%29.jpg/500px-Roger_Melen_at_the_Oasis_%282018%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roger Melen, who suggested meeting here, at the Oasis","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoger_Melen_at_the_Oasis_(2018).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.w